<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Guild Dues and the Unlicensed Crafter
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Village Smith's Real Economy
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>The monthly-demand tables later in this chapter are blunt about villages: there simply is not enough buying power in a community of five hundred people to sustain a full-time weapon-and-armor crafter. A village smith who tried to live on new longsword sales would starve.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Real village smiths did not. They survived because they were not, functionally, weapon-and-armor crafters. They were the village's blacksmith, which meant: they shod horses, repaired plow-blades, forged nails by the barrel, fixed hinges, fixed kettles, and sometimes fixed swords. They held a small plot of land worked by their family. They accepted payment in eggs, cheese, and labor as often as in coin.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A player character who wants to run a village smithing operation should assume this model: roughly half the income comes from repair and horseshoeing (not covered in detail by this book, but a reasonable GM estimate is 15<0x2013>20 gp per month of steady work), a quarter from farming or foraging (effectively halving the lifestyle cost), and a quarter from occasional new-production sales. The crafting rules above still apply to the new-production portion; the rest is color. Set repair income much higher than 20 gp/month and the village smith starts outearning town artisans <0x2014> which undermines the book's implicit premise that towns are the prestige path for crafting as a career.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A pure new-production smith belongs in a town or larger. The rural smith belongs in a story.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Foreign Crafters in Guild Towns
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A crafter passing through a guild town with goods made elsewhere is generally permitted to sell at a market stall for a fee <0x2014> typically one day's sale at 15% of gross. Goods demonstrably made in the host town by an outsider are another matter entirely and typically require either guild membership or a very convincing story.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
