<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading3>Per-Zone Durability
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading3>Playing Without Piecemeal
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Called Shots Without Piecemeal
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Groups skipping the piecemeal system still benefit from a lightweight called-shot rule: when an attacker targets a visibly unarmored zone (a helmetless head, a bare forearm), apply a Moderate penalty to the strike; on a hit, the armor provides no benefit on that strike. This preserves the feel of piecemeal without the bookkeeping.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Soft Armor <0x2014> Minimal Tier
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Silk and Other Surprises
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Historical Mongol and Chinese light cavalry wore silk under their armor. Silk does not stop an arrow <0x2014> but if the arrow penetrated cloth and flesh, the silk would wrap around the arrowhead rather than tearing, allowing the wound to be cleaned and the arrow withdrawn cleanly rather than pushed through. Groups wanting to model this can treat silk as granting a Slight bonus to recovery rolls from arrow wounds.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A full silk shirt appropriate to this use runs 40 gp and weighs practically nothing, but acquiring one outside a trading city is difficult.
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Light Armor <0x2014> Light Tier
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Medium Armor <0x2014> Medium Tier
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Heavy Armor <0x2014> Heavy Tier
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Extreme Armor <0x2014> Extreme Tier
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Fantasy Variant: Dwarven Plate
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Dwarven-forged plate of any tier gains a Moderate Durability bonus, a Slight weight reduction, and a characteristic "ringing" note when struck <0x2014> the sound is used by dwarven smiths as a quality test. A set of dwarven full plate runs 8,000 gp (Major cost bonus over human work) and takes approximately three times the baseline labor. Dwarven artisans refuse to sell to non-dwarven buyers in some settings.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Fantasy Variant: Elven Moon-Silver Mail
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A chain hauberk woven from moon-silver rings retains the full Protection of steel chain while reducing weight to 12 lbs and bulk to 1. The wearer gains a Slight Agility bonus. The armor glows faintly in moonlight (ten-foot dim radius) and cannot be repaired outside an elven forge. Cost: 3,000 gp (Severe bonus over human work).
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Fantasy Variant: Dragon-Bone Scale
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A scale coat built from calcified dragon-bone scales. Protection tier matches lamellar (Medium), but weight is 25 lbs, and the armor carries the elemental affinity of its source dragon <0x2014> fire-drake scale grants Moderate fire resistance; frost-drake, cold; lightning, electrical. Cost: varies with source, starting at 2,500 gp and climbing. Many dragons consider wearing their kin's scale a provocation worth hunting over.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Helmets
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Shields
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading3>Shield Mechanics in Brief
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading3>Buckler
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading3>Small and Medium Shields
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading3>Large Shields
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading3>Historical Curiosities
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Kalkan <0x2014> The Arrow-Eater
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A light shield of concentric woven reeds bound with silk, with a central steel boss. Used by Crimean Tartars and Polish light cavalry through the 17th century. Far more effective against arrows than its light weight would suggest: the flexible reed absorbs the force of a thrust rather than breaking.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Parry:<CharStyle:> Moderate · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability:<CharStyle:> 40 · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 3 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 1 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 90 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Moderate bonus vs. arrows and crossbow bolts. Slight Durability penalty against heavy edged weapons.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Hungarian Tartsche <0x2014> The Winged Shield
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>An asymmetrical shield with a tall "wing" extending up one side, designed specifically to protect the rider's neck and shoulder from lances and sabers while not obstructing the rider's sword arm.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Parry:<CharStyle:> Moderate · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability:<CharStyle:> 70 · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 6 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 2 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 140 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Moderate bonus against attacks targeting the head or neck.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Lantern-Shield
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>An Italian experimental design: a buckler with a shuttered lantern mounted at its center, a serrated "sword-breaker" edge along the rim, and a hook for catching bridle reins.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Parry:<CharStyle:> Slight · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability:<CharStyle:> 40 · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 4 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 1 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 250 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Lantern shuttered closed: normal buckler. Lantern unshuttered: any opponent directly in front must make a Willpower check at Challenging difficulty or be Dazzled (Moderate penalty to attack rolls for one round). Serrated edge attempts Disarm at Slight bonus. Reliably malfunctions in rain.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Zertsalo <0x2014> The Mirror-Plate
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Not a shield held in the hand, but a large circular polished steel plate strapped over the center of the chest, as a reinforcement layer over a mail or brigandine suit. A Russian and Persian tradition; the polished surface was both practical (deflecting arrows) and ceremonial.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Coverage:<CharStyle:> Torso (upgrade) · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability Bonus:<CharStyle:> +20 to torso · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 4 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 1 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 140 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> When worn over existing medium-or-heavier armor, upgrades torso Protection by a Slight bonus. Slight Presence bonus (the polished steel is impressive). In direct sunlight, a character with a clear line of sight to the mirror must make a Willpower check at Routine difficulty or be Dazzled (Slight Perception penalty for one round).
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Zertsalo-Shield <0x2014> The Mirror Array
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A theoretical siege shield mounted with rows of small polished steel plates, designed to reflect the sun into the eyes of defenders on a castle wall. Historically almost never used, because the wielder became the most obvious target on the entire battlefield.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Parry:<CharStyle:> Slight · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability:<CharStyle:> 90 · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 14 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 3 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 300 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> In bright sunlight and with a clear line of sight to the enemy position, enemies attacking from the front suffer a Moderate penalty to Strike for as long as the wielder stands still. The wielder is considered Visible <0x2014> any ranged attack against them, including by allies of the intended targets, gains a Slight bonus. Only useful in very specific conditions; rarely practical.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Tarkh <0x2014> The Gun-Shield
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A heavy iron buckler or round shield with a built-in matchlock pistol barrel protruding through the boss. When the shield was pointed at the target, the wielder could fire the single ball before closing to melee.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Parry:<CharStyle:> Slight · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability:<CharStyle:> 50 · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 10 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 2 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 400 gp (plus ammunition)
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Contains a single-shot smoothbore barrel (Solid damage on a hit; range limited to 30 feet before accuracy collapses). Reload requires 3 actions and a secure position. Historically, recoil bruised the wielder's arm badly enough that the weapon was rarely used twice in the same engagement. Moderate Weight penalty and Slight Agility penalty due to bulk.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Bronze Heraldic Shield
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A ceremonial variant of any wooden shield, faced with hammered bronze and ornately decorated with embossed heraldry. Offers no mechanical advantage over a painted wooden shield, but signals noble bearing. Worn into tournament and ceremonial combat; rarely taken to war.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Parry:<CharStyle:> As base shield · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability:<CharStyle:> As base · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> +2 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> +0 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> +Major cost bonus
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Moderate Presence bonus in social and ceremonial contexts. Moderate Durability penalty against heavy blows (the bronze face dents visibly).
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
