<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading1>Chapter 5: Blades & Bludgeons
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>How to Read This Chapter
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Weapon Traits
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Knives & Daggers
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Short Blades
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>One-Handed Swords
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Two-Handed Swords
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Axes
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Maces & Hammers
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Flails
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Specialty & Exotic Weapons
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Historical Curiosities
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Tsar's Gauntlet
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A theoretical design recorded in Russian metalworking manuals: an armored gauntlet with a spring-loaded stiletto concealed inside the forearm casing. A small trigger in the palm released the blade, allowing a seemingly-unarmed wielder to deliver a close-range strike. No confirmed working examples are known to have been produced.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> Glancing (if trigger succeeds) · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> +2 lbs on the gauntlet · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 300 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> A hidden strike; no Stealth penalty to reveal. On the first round of a surprise attack, grants a Moderate bonus to Strike. Triggers jam on a failed Agility check at Routine difficulty.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Hook-Sword (Shuang Gou)
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A Chinese paired weapon: two curved swords with hooks at the point and crescent guards at the hilt. Can be interlocked at the hooks to extend reach or catch an opponent's weapon.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> Heavy · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 3 lbs each · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 250 gp the pair
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Finesse, Hooking, Entangling. When linked: Reach (Moderate) and Moderate bonus to Disarm. Users without specific training suffer a Major penalty.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Kukri
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A curved, forward-angled short blade used as both tool and weapon throughout the Himalayan region. The forward-angled geometry concentrates the force of the cut toward the blade's belly, producing chops harder than a similar-mass straight blade can deliver.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> Solid (+Slight vs. unarmored) · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 1.5 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 0 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 35 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Quick, Brutal. Can be thrown (Range 15 feet). Functions as a utility tool without penalty (skinning, chopping wood).
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Macuahuitl
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A Mesoamerican sword-club of wood inlaid with obsidian edges. A pre-conquest weapon capable of decapitating a horse in a single well-struck blow, at the cost of losing its obsidian edges after a few engagements.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> Heavy (Crushing on first strike of combat) · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 4 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 2 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 60 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> First strike of each combat ignores one Protection tier (obsidian edge). Each further strike risks breaking an edge <0x2014> on any natural failure, Slight Durability penalty until re-edged. Re-edging requires obsidian flakes and a day of work.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Atgeir (Saga Polearm-Sword)
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A Norse hybrid weapon of uncertain exact form, referenced in the sagas as a weapon halfway between a spear and a sword. Modern interpretations suggest a long-bladed spear useful for both thrusting and cutting.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> Heavy · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> 5 lbs · <CharStyle:Bold>Bulk:<CharStyle:> 3 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 120 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Two-Handed, Reach (Slight), Versatile between thrust and cut. A skilled wielder can switch grips to use it either as a thrusting spear or a cutting sword.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Fantasy Variants
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Dwarven Axe
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A battle axe or great axe forged in the dwarven tradition gains a Moderate Durability bonus, a Slight damage bonus, and the characteristic ringing tone when struck against armor. Dwarven axes come in standard and two-handed sizes; the signature "singer's edge" is a mirror-polished bevel that can be used to test the harmony of a blade by tone.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Cost: +Major bonus to base axe price (×3).
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Elven Moon-Silver Blade
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A sword or dagger woven from moon-silver rather than steel. Retains all silver's supernatural properties (bypassing lycanthrope and certain fae resistances) while carrying a Slight damage bonus and Major weight reduction. Glows faintly in moonlight.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Cost: +Severe bonus to base weapon price (×10).
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Dragon-Bone Weapon
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A weapon carved from dragon-bone rather than forged from metal. Carries a Slight damage bonus, a Moderate weight reduction, and the elemental affinity of its source <0x2014> fire-drake weapons deal additional Slight fire damage, frost-drake cold, and so on.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Cost: +Severe bonus to base weapon price.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A dragon whose kin's bone is wielded openly in its territory will not take kindly to the wielder. Some settings consider dragon-bone weapons cursed outright.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Rune-Bound Weapons
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Any weapon of sufficient quality can have a single rune bound into it, granting a specific effect per Chapter 19's rules. A rune-bound weapon of otherwise standard construction becomes the beginning of a legendary item <0x2014> but only the beginning. Most named legendary blades in myth are rune-bound masterworks of specific exotic materials.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Cost for standard rune-binding: +Major bonus (×3) on top of the base weapon. See Chapter 19 for the full process and effects.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
