<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading1>Chapter 6: Polearms & Long Weapons
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Why Polearms
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>How Polearms Work
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Spears
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Hewing Polearms
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Pick & Beak Polearms
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Specialty Polearms
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Historical Curiosities
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Flamberge Pike
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A pike with a wavy, fire-edged head <0x2014> the pike equivalent of the flamberge sword. The waves produced prolonged-bleeding wounds when a pikeman managed to withdraw after a thrust, which was rarer than you might think. Mostly ceremonial; parade weapons for elite guards.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> as pike, plus Slight per-wound bleed for one scene · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> +Major cost bonus (×3)
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> as pike, plus wounds bleed (see flamberge sword, Chapter 5).
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Triple-Head Pike
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A late-renaissance experimental design with three parallel pike-heads on a single shaft, intended to triple the chance of finding armor gaps at a charge. In practice, the added weight made it difficult to hold steady, and the heads deflected one another rather than compensating for each other's misses. Rare outside museum collections.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> as pike · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> +Moderate cost bonus · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> +Moderate
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Slight bonus to hit on a braced charge; Moderate Agility penalty at all other times.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Gun-Spear
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A matchlock or wheellock pistol mechanism built into the shaft of a long spear, allowing a single shot to be fired before the spear was committed to melee. Similar concept to the gun-shield (Chapter 4), and with similar problems: the mechanism added weight, the recoil was unbalanced, and the mechanism was vulnerable to the blow that the spear was about to deliver.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> as short spear (melee), Solid (fired) · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 350 gp · <CharStyle:Bold>Weight:<CharStyle:> +3 lbs
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> as short spear, plus single shot (Solid damage, 30 ft range, 3 actions to reload).
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Fantasy Variants
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Dwarven Halberd
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A halberd forged by dwarven smiths gains a Moderate Durability bonus and a Slight damage bonus, with the characteristic ringing tone common to all dwarven work. Dwarven halberds are typically inherited through families and never sold; the fact that one is available for purchase tells a story.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Cost: +Major cost bonus over standard halberd.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Dragon-Bone Pike
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A pike carved from dragon-bone rather than forged from steel. Carries the elemental affinity of the source dragon (see Chapter 2); a fire-drake pike deals an additional Slight fire damage, frost-drake cold, etc. Major weight reduction makes it usable by smaller characters who could not normally wield a pike.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Cost: +Severe cost bonus.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Living-Wood Stave
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A two-handed fighting staff made of living wood (see Chapter 2). Not a true weapon so much as a druid's tool, but a skilled wielder can use it as a quarterstaff with the Versatile trait and a Slight damage bonus. Self-repairs overnight when rested against the earth; cannot be lit on fire.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Cost: +Major cost bonus over a standard quarterstaff.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>The Quarterstaff
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Using Polearms in Specific Situations
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
