<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading1>Chapter 12: Siege Engines & Fortifications
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>What This Chapter Covers
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Structural Damage Scale
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Battering Rams
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Throwing Engines
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Shooting Engines
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Siege Towers and Bridges
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Mining and Sapping
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Fortifications
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Defensive Curiosities
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Fire-Wheel
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A massive wooden wheel, filled with straw, pitch, and sulfur, lit on fire and rolled down a slope toward an attacking force or siege engine camp. Used mostly in Russian and mountain defensive traditions.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> Crushing × 3 (impact) + Heavy (fire per round) · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability:<CharStyle:> 100 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 80 gp
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Must be deployed on a slope of at least 10°. 30% chance per 100 yards of rolling off-course (wind, terrain). Burns out in 5 rounds. Can hit friendly forces if terrain changes mid-roll.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Fire-Pig
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>An actual pig (or several) covered in pitch and set alight, driven toward enemy siege works. A desperate tactic historically associated with defenders facing imminent defeat. Unpredictable, unreliable, and occasionally effective.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Damage:<CharStyle:> 3d6 (crushing) + 3d6 (fire per round that contact is maintained)
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Animals panic unpredictably. Roll 1d8 each round for direction. 30% chance per round of running the wrong way. If it reaches a wooden siege structure, deals 3d6 fire damage per round until the pig collapses (1d4 rounds). Also: you now have to live with the fact that you set a pig on fire.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>The Wall-Crane (Archimedes' Claw)
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>A large hooked crane mounted on a harbor wall, designed to snag attacking ships and capsize them. Theoretical in many settings; documented in specific classical histories (the defense of Syracuse).
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Range:<CharStyle:> 60 ft from wall · <CharStyle:Bold>Durability:<CharStyle:> 400 · <CharStyle:Bold>Cost:<CharStyle:> 2,500 gp + permanent emplacement
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody><CharStyle:Bold>Traits:<CharStyle:> Operator rolls Strength at Challenging difficulty. On success, ship is grappled and can be winched <0x2014> Strength contest with Strength 50 (the crane) vs. the ship's Strength (varies, large ships are Strength 20+). Winning the contest pulls the ship toward the wall or capsizes it.
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000E>
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarHeading>Greek Fire (Wall Emplacement)
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>The defensive variant of the Greek Fire siphon from Chapter 11, mounted on a fortification rather than a ship. Same mechanics; used against approaching siege engines, troops, or ships attacking a harbor fortification.
<ParaStyle:Sidebars:SidebarBody>Damage and cost as per Chapter 11.
<ParaStyle:Headings:Heading2>Siege Logistics
<ParaStyle:Body Text:CoreBody><0x000C>
